# Biomes and World Climate Regions: The Great Pattern of Life Zones

> Why do equatorial forests steam with life while high latitudes
> wear lichen and ice? The answer is a chain: sun → temperature and
> rainfall regimes → soils and vegetation → animals and human land
> use. That chain produces the world's great **biomes** — repeated,
> predictable life zones that appear wherever the climate recipe is
> found, whether in Africa, Asia, or the Americas.

## 1. Climate Controls: The Recipe Before the Cake

[definition]
A **biome** is a large, regionally distinct community of plants and
animals shaped mainly by climate (temperature and precipitation) and
secondarily by soils, relief, and human modification. A **climate
region** is the physical classification of the same territory — the
two maps largely coincide.
[/definition]

The controlling ingredients:

- **Latitude** governs the annual solar dose and seasonality — the
  fundamental control.
- **Pressure and wind belts** deliver moisture: rising air at the
  equator (wet), sinking at the tropics (dry), stormy mid-latitude
  westerlies.
- **Continentality:** interiors swing between temperature extremes;
  coasts stay mild.
- **Altitude** mimics latitude — a tropical mountain runs from hot
  lowland to cold "alpine tundra" within kilometres.
- **Ocean currents and rain shadows** redistribute heat and moisture
  (cold coasts desiccate, warm coasts rain).
- **Monsoon dynamics** override the general circulation over South
  and East Asia with seasonal reversal.

## 2. The Köppen Alphabet (Read Any Climate Code)

Wladimir Köppen's classification labels every climate with letters
worth decoding on exam maps:

| First letter | Second letter (temperature) | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **A** tropical | f = wet year-round, m = monsoon, s = dry summer, w = dry winter | **Af** — equatorial rainforest |
| **B** dry | S = steppe, W = desert (h/k for hot/cold) | **BWh** — hot desert |
| **C** temperate | s = dry summer, w = dry winter, f = wet | **Csb** — Mediterranean |
| **D** continental | s/w/f as above | **Dfb** — humid continental |
| **E** polar | T = tundra, F = ice cap | **ET** — tundra |
| H added for highland | — | **H** — Alpine climates |

## 3. The Great Biomes from Equator to Pole

**Equatorial rainforest (Af).** 2000+ mm rain all year, no true
season. Leached **latosols** (nutrients locked in living biomass, not
soil), buttress-rooted hardwoods, four canopy storeys, the planet's
richest biodiversity (Congo, Amazon, Indonesia). The dark, humid
floor is surprisingly quiet and nutrient-thin.

**Tropical monsoon and savanna (Am/Aw).** A wet season traded
against a fierce dry one — grassland with scattered drought-resistant
trees (baobab, acacia) and the great herd migrations of the Serengeti
and the Terai. Red **laterite** soils, fire-adapted vegetation, and
dense human pressure (this is much of India and Africa's farm belt).

**Hot deserts (BWh).** Sub-tropical high pressure plus cold-current
coasts cap rainfall below 250 mm: cacti/succulents (New World),
xerophytic shrubs (Old World), camel thorn, salt pans and shifting
sand seas, nomadic herding and oasis life (Sahara, Thar, Atacama —
Earth's driest).

**Mediterranean (Cs).** Dry sunny summers, mild wet winters; the
**maquis/chaparral** scrub of thick, small, waxy leaves; orchard
agriculture — olives, grapes, citrus — on the western flanks of
continents (California, Cape, coastal Mediterranean lands).

**Humid subtropical (Cfa) and humid continental (Dfb).** The monsoon-
like summer rains of eastern seaboards support paddy, tea, and
deciduous hardwood; further poleward, conifer-mixed forests meet
fertile prairie and chernozem grasslands — the world's breadbaskets
(Ukraine, North American Midwest, North China Plain).

**Taiga / boreal forest (Dfc).** The vast cold conifer belt of
spruce-pine-fir, podzol soils, short growing seasons, and paper-and-
timber economies (Siberia, Canada, Fennoscandia) — the largest land
biome on Earth.

**Tundra (ET) and ice caps (EF).** Permafrost, matted lichens,
cushion plants, lemming–caribou–wolf chains in the brief polar
summer; poleward of that, permanent ice (Greenland, Antarctica).

